irqchip/gic-v3-its: Claim LPI tables inherited across kexec on DT

When a kernel is kexec'd into while LPIs are enabled, the incoming kernel
finds GICR_CTLR.EnableLPIs set and tries to re-initialise the LPI tables
from scratch.  That requires disabling LPIs first, which is not always
possible: on some implementations (e.g. Qualcomm SM8750) GICR_CTLR.EnableLPIs
is RES1 once set, the write is ignored and redist_disable_lpis() returns
-EBUSY.  its_cpu_init() then bails out early, LPIs are never reprogrammed,
and every MSI command queued against the ITS waits for its completion
timeout: measured 99 timeouts of ~1.25 s each, pushing /init out from
1.3 s to 126 s.

The tables themselves are still intact and usable, and the driver already
has a path for that (RDIST_FLAGS_RD_TABLES_PREALLOCATED), but it is gated on
the tables being reserved memory.  On an EFI boot the outgoing kernel records
a persistent reservation; on a DT boot nothing does.  fdt_add_mem_rsv() is not
an option either: the flattened DTB handed over by the bootloader has no space
left for a new entry (-FDT_ERR_NOSPACE), and kexec clones that blob when the
image is loaded, long before the jump.

So claim the inherited tables in the incoming kernel instead: reserve the
property table while LPIs are being probed (memblock still owns the pages, well
before memblock_free_all()), and the per-CPU pending tables when the
preallocated path is taken.  The preallocated path is then selected normally
and the ITS keeps working across the handoff.

Measured on SM8750 (kexec into the same image, disk root):

  before: 99 x "ITS queue timeout", 8 x "Failed to disable LPIs", /init 126.35 s
  after:  0 x "ITS queue timeout",  0 x "Failed to disable LPIs", /init   1.31 s

Behaviour on a normal cold boot is unchanged: enabled_lpis_allowed() is only
consulted when LPIs are already enabled on entry, and the preallocated path is
only taken when that flag is set.

Signed-off-by: xuqianhui <xiaqinghuan@mail.ustc.edu.cn>

diff --git a/drivers/irqchip/irq-gic-v3-its.c b/drivers/irqchip/irq-gic-v3-its.c
index 6f5811aae5..2c23da11b2 100644
--- a/drivers/irqchip/irq-gic-v3-its.c
+++ b/drivers/irqchip/irq-gic-v3-its.c
@@ -2280,7 +2280,7 @@ static void its_free_prop_table(struct page *prop_page)
 	its_free_pages(page_address(prop_page), get_order(LPI_PROPBASE_SZ));
 }
 
-static bool gic_check_reserved_range(phys_addr_t addr, unsigned long size)
+static bool gic_range_is_reserved(phys_addr_t addr, unsigned long size)
 {
 	phys_addr_t start, end, addr_end;
 	u64 i;
@@ -2300,9 +2300,17 @@ static bool gic_check_reserved_range(phys_addr_t addr, unsigned long size)
 			return true;
 	}
 
+	return false;
+}
+
+static bool gic_check_reserved_range(phys_addr_t addr, unsigned long size)
+{
+	if (gic_range_is_reserved(addr, size))
+		return true;
+
 	/* Not found, not a good sign... */
 	pr_warn("GICv3: Expected reserved range [%pa:%pa], not found\n",
-		&addr, &addr_end);
+		&addr, &addr + size - 1);
 	add_taint(TAINT_CRAP, LOCKDEP_STILL_OK);
 	return false;
 }
@@ -3067,7 +3075,26 @@ static bool enabled_lpis_allowed(void)
 	val = gicr_read_propbaser(gic_data_rdist_rd_base() + GICR_PROPBASER);
 	addr = val & GENMASK_ULL(51, 12);
 
-	return gic_check_reserved_range(addr, LPI_PROPBASE_SZ);
+	if (gic_range_is_reserved(addr, LPI_PROPBASE_SZ))
+		return true;
+
+	/*
+	 * LPIs are already enabled, but the tables we inherited are not marked
+	 * reserved.  On a DT boot there is no EFI persistent reservation and the
+	 * flattened DTB has no room left for a memreserve entry, so the kernel we
+	 * came from (kexec) had no way to hand them over as reserved.
+	 *
+	 * We cannot start from scratch either: PROPBASER/PENDBASER may only be
+	 * reprogrammed with LPIs disabled, and on some implementations
+	 * GICR_CTLR.EnableLPIs reads as RES1 once set.  So keep the inherited
+	 * tables and claim them here, before memblock hands the pages over to the
+	 * buddy allocator.
+	 */
+	if (memblock_reserve(addr, LPI_PROPBASE_SZ))
+		return false;
+
+	pr_info("GICv3: claiming inherited LPI property table @%pa\n", &addr);
+	return true;
 }
 
 static int __init allocate_lpi_tables(void)
@@ -3175,7 +3202,14 @@ static void its_cpu_init_lpis(void)
 		paddr = gicr_read_pendbaser(rbase + GICR_PENDBASER);
 		paddr &= GENMASK_ULL(51, 16);
 
-		WARN_ON(!gic_check_reserved_range(paddr, LPI_PENDBASE_SZ));
+		/*
+		 * Same as the property table: keep the inherited pending table
+		 * rather than handing the ITS a range the allocator may reuse.
+		 */
+		if (!gic_range_is_reserved(paddr, LPI_PENDBASE_SZ)) {
+			memblock_reserve(paddr, LPI_PENDBASE_SZ);
+			pr_info_once("GICv3: claiming inherited LPI pending tables\n");
+		}
 		gic_data_rdist()->flags |= RD_LOCAL_PENDTABLE_PREALLOCATED;
 
 		goto out;
